1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,860
Hello, my name is CHAN HIO WENG, and this is my project demo.

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:10,980
Its name is FreshCall. Let me explain it step by step first. So here is the main page.

3
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:15,780
Here you can see what the store manager actually sees,

4
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:20,200
and this sidebar is only for demo use. First of all, let me explain my motivation.

5
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:22,220
The project comes from fresh food businesses,

6
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:24,980
like supermarkets and fast food restaurants,

7
00:00:25,020 --> 00:00:28,940
where food that isn't sold soon is thrown away. Let's imagine that every night,

8
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:34,460
a restaurant or store manager orders tomorrow's fresh food, about a dozen items,

9
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:39,980
even a hundred, every day, usually by copying what sold on the same day last week.

10
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:45,040
However, copying last week gives one number, with no sense of how far to trust it.

11
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:50,860
And my product, FreshCall, was created to fix this problem.

12
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:54,740
It gives the manager an order for every item in whole cases,

13
00:00:54,780 --> 00:01:01,260
and a likely range for the items that need judgement by the manager.

14
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:04,800
Next, I will introduce the dataset that I use for this project.

15
00:01:04,840 --> 00:01:09,800
The dataset is Kaggle's Favorita dataset,

16
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:16,720
a real supermarket chain in Ecuador, about 125 million daily sales rows.

17
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:20,300
And here is what the raw data looks like.

18
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:24,980
Here is the date, store number, item number, its category and its sales.

19
00:01:26,360 --> 00:01:32,120
So I kept the items that can be ordered and forecast honestly first.

20
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:39,670
And here are the steps. That leaves the perishable items,

21
00:01:39,710 --> 00:01:45,120
sold in whole units so they fit in cases, sold on most days, with enough history.

22
00:01:45,160 --> 00:01:50,360
Finally, about 430 items per store.

23
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:57,480
And here's a rule for every result of this project: I built each design on store 44,

24
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:02,040
then confirmed it once on a store I had never looked at,

25
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:05,680
like store 49, store 8 and store 45.

26
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:09,840
So which kind of AI fits this project?

27
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:14,120
The core job is a numeric forecast with years of sales history behind it.

28
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:17,000
That's why I chose narrow machine learning, quantile regression,

29
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:21,660
because instead of a single guess, it predicts a range of tomorrow's demand.

30
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:26,840
On the other hand, I deliberately kept the language model away from the order itself.

31
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:29,740
First, it can't give me a range that I can actually test.

32
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:34,060
Second, because LLMs sample their answers, it doesn't give the same answer twice.

33
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:36,700
Finally, to judge all of this fairly,

34
00:02:36,740 --> 00:02:39,660
my baseline is simply what most managers do today,

35
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:44,720
which is copying last week's number, and every result is measured against it.

36
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:48,120
Okay, let me explain my first design, and why I dropped it.

37
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:52,280
My first design ordered only when the model was sure, and said "ASK ME" otherwise.

38
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:57,520
The first version, v1, was no better than random, and the second worked on store 49,

39
00:02:57,560 --> 00:03:02,320
but handed back three quarters of the items.

40
00:03:02,360 --> 00:03:06,760
For the results, I measured what a manager actually feels on store 44,

41
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:12,480
per forty items a night. Copying last week gives 15.5 wrong orders.

42
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:17,400
The ASK ME design gives 15.2, and about 16 minutes of the manager's time.

43
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:24,100
Letting the model answer everything gives 13.1 wrong orders in about 3 minutes.

44
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:26,360
But the lesson is that handing an item

45
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:31,110
to someone with no extra information makes the order worse.

46
00:03:31,150 --> 00:03:36,980
So in fact, the first design didn't give us an ideal result, so I redesigned it.

47
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:40,320
Here's my latest design of FreshCall, version 3.

48
00:03:40,360 --> 00:03:43,720
We're using store 8's data for confirmation.

49
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:48,740
So here is what the manager sees in FreshCall: every item has an order.

50
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:54,000
The bar here is the likely range in cases. The dot is the system's order.

51
00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:59,260
The wider the bar, the less sure the model, so those rows come first,

52
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:04,380
and the details give a one-line reason.

53
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:07,280
The slow items that never sold more than a case a day

54
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:12,180
in the last four weeks are folded into standing orders.

55
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:18,000
So does it work? Compared with copying last week, on the items that need judgement,

56
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:23,040
wrong orders, meaning the wrong number of cases,

57
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:27,993
fell from 13.3 to 10.3 per forty items.

58
00:04:28,033 --> 00:04:33,060
Units wasted fell 11%, and shortages 26%.

59
00:04:33,100 --> 00:04:36,560
It beat last week in all three test months.

60
00:04:36,600 --> 00:04:42,320
Then, what this doesn't show: it assumes the manager accepts every suggestion,

61
00:04:42,360 --> 00:04:46,560
so a manager who knows something extra could do better still.

62
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:53,340
And on the slow items, the model wasted more than last week to avoid running out.

63
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:57,840
Whether that's worth it depends on what a shortage costs the store,

64
00:04:57,880 --> 00:05:02,467
which is the project's next stage, or next step.

65
00:05:02,507 --> 00:05:08,340
Moreover, as we can see, here is the cost ratio,

66
00:05:08,380 --> 00:05:16,020
and waste versus stockout is a business choice, so I made it a setting:

67
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:20,980
this cost ratio means how much worse running out is than wasting.

68
00:05:21,020 --> 00:05:26,320
So the system picks the number of cases with the lowest expected cost:

69
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:31,690
the newsvendor idea. Slide right, it orders more;

70
00:05:31,730 --> 00:05:35,640
slide left, it orders less. According to the results,

71
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:41,000
my first version of this rule actually lost on my development stores,

72
00:05:41,040 --> 00:05:45,940
because aiming at a higher forecast and then rounding up to whole cases over-ordered.

73
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:52,120
Then I fixed it, and registered the new test before touching store 45.

74
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:56,100
There, it beat the old order at every ratio:

75
00:05:56,140 --> 00:06:02,560
6, 4 and 18% cheaper at ratios 2, 4 and 9.

76
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:05,200
One caution is that at lower ratios,

77
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:09,680
the savings look huge, but mostly because the system orders nothing,

78
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:14,880
and that's partly an artifact of my data having no stock carried over.

79
00:06:14,920 --> 00:06:16,920
So I only quote two and above.

80
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:22,070
So overall, every number comes from my scripts.

81
00:06:22,110 --> 00:06:27,360
This one re-scores store 45 right now,

82
00:06:27,400 --> 00:06:31,080
printing the verdict I registered in git before the result existed.

83
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:34,920
I also leaked the answer into the model on purpose:

84
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:42,792
its lead over last week jumped from 11.8% to 20.7%,

85
00:06:42,832 --> 00:06:48,720
a fake pass of my 15% target, and my unit tests catch that bug.

86
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:55,590
So overall, my project, FreshCall, is a tool to provide advice.

87
00:06:55,630 --> 00:06:58,860
It never submits an order and shows no confidence score,

88
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:03,700
so the manager confirms every line,

89
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:07,320
following Singapore's IMDA framework for human oversight.

90
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:13,300
Not built: detecting one-off events like a local promotion.

91
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:18,560
And honestly, this is supermarket data with no stock records,

92
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:25,420
so I validated forecasting, how uncertainty is shown and the ordering rule,

93
00:07:25,460 --> 00:07:31,660
not ordering with real inventory. So the case size and timings are all assumptions.

94
00:07:31,700 --> 00:07:37,000
So to conclude, I began with a system designed to hold back when it was uncertain,

95
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:40,080
and the evidence led me away from that design.

96
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:45,770
So the final result is simpler and more useful,

97
00:07:45,810 --> 00:07:50,720
which is an order for every item, sized to each store's own costs,

98
00:07:50,760 --> 00:07:57,880
with every number reproducible from the repository. Thank you.

